<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Spec 31 §3/§5: the macOS runtime signing profile (fixed set, pinned
  here until spec 31 §2's manifest derivation lands).

  allow-dyld-environment-variables — THE LOAD-BEARING GRANT
    (tebako#1117; the tebako-runtime-r#5 precedent). The spec 22
    tier-1 visibility design re-execs the exe with the driver-armed
    DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES (build/resources/tebako_python_main.c's
    second incarnation — the in-image libtfs_preload.dylib is the
    UNPATCHED CPython interpreter's only VFS visibility). The
    hardened runtime strips every DYLD_* variable at launch unless
    this grant is present; the re-exec'd incarnation then runs
    shimless, the mount root (/__tfs__) does not exist on the host
    filesystem, and the interpreter dies "ModuleNotFoundError: No
    module named 'encodings'" — python's exact analog of R's
    "unable to open the base package" (tebako-runtime-r#5). Proven
    on darwin-23 (macOS 14.1.1 arm64) against the published
    pair's own class: the same exe adhoc-signed with the hardened
    runtime and the two-key profile dies on the encodings import;
    re-signed adding ONLY this grant, the boot serves stdlib imports
    and a libc open() of an in-image path. Every published python runtime
    to date ships UNSIGNED (APPLE_SIGNING_ENABLED has never been
    armed in this repo), and dyld strips the insertion only for
    hardened-runtime targets — the only reason the omission never
    fired (tebako#1117's "works on darwin-23" cell). The macos-15
    runners TOLERATE a missing grant (their dyld keeps the
    variables), so no CI runner can police this class at exec time:
    ci/macos-sign-notarize-runtime.sh asserts the embedded profile
    statically, by key name — this grant must be embedded BEFORE
    the signing gate ever arms, which is why it lands while the
    legs still ship unsigned.
  disable-library-validation — EVERY runtime slice: a hardened-runtime
    process by default refuses unsigned dylibs, and the driver
    materializes in-image native extensions (.so) to the host for
    dlopen; they carry no Developer ID signature of their own (§1.4).
    This grant governs only WHICH dylibs may load — it says nothing
    about WHETHER dyld honors the environment; only the grant above
    does (the pair is not redundant).
  allow-jit — CPython JIT builds (the matrix's jit_llvm legs) map
    executable memory at runtime (MAP_JIT on arm64). Harmless on a
    python built without a JIT: the entitlement is a grant, never a
    requirement.

  Keep each <true/> on its own line: the embedding check in
  ci/macos-sign-notarize-runtime.sh counts them AND greps each key
  name (macOS 15 emits the embedded plist single-line).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
